task: dataflow-coordinator
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-coordinator
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfc1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 8

why: every stage since 6a ends with the same sentence — if the
  COORDINATOR dies the run dies with it. It holds the folded state
  and journals nothing, and no amount of worker recovery helps. It is
  also the exact boundary stage 6c had to draw around exactly-once:
  within a run the pane's identity is enough, ACROSS runs a restarted
  coordinator re-offers everything.

what: the coordinator's state as a VALUE, and a journal to put it in.

  1. `Wire.state: Schema[S]` — the coordinator's running state
     described the same way `wire: Schema[W]` describes a partial.
     Every S in the file is already a value in disguise: a fold's is
     its accumulator, a keyed sink's is a map of accumulators, a
     windowed sink's is the open panes plus what has been retired.
     `Schema.SIso` carries the mutable ones, as it already carries
     stage 7's terminals.

  2. `Checkpoint` — save(epoch, bytes) / latest — an injectable seam,
     NOT a dependency. okay-cluster's compile graph stays at
     okay-codec; okay-persist joins in TEST scope and proves the
     assembly against the real log, the way okay-tls already joins
     okay-persist's tests.

  3. `Cluster.stream` commits after each epoch's absorb and resumes
     from the last committed one. The workers need nothing new: 6b's
     `Advance` already carries the epoch INDEX, so a replacement
     replays to it — a resumed coordinator is a replacement for
     everyone at once.

the argument that this is small: the epoch loop is LOCK-STEP. Every
  partition contributes exactly rounds 1..N before the coordinator
  folds, so a checkpoint taken after absorbing round N is consistent
  by construction. There is no alignment protocol to write, no
  barrier to inject, and nothing to reconcile — which is the whole
  reason the epoch loop was built that way in 6a.

predict: the mechanism lands small and the honest sentence is again
  the hard part. The window between WRITING a pane and COMMITTING the
  epoch is at-least-once, so 6c's promise across runs is the same
  promise it is within one — an outcome, on a keyed writer — and the
  spec has to say where the window is rather than claim there is none.
